    {
      "title": "As New Zealand polls close, results expected to be tightest in years",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/as-new-zealand-polls-close-results-expected-to-be-tightest-in-years-126110700384_1.html",
      "description": "Polling places closed on Saturday after the final day of voting in New Zealand's election following a tense campaign that left the main contenders neck and neck with no clear route to victory for either.\nAn incumbent conservative government led by the National party is seeking a second term against a leftwing bloc led by Labour.\nIn polls close to election day, neither Prime Minister Christopher Luxon of National nor opposition leader Chris Hipkins of Labour had a clear path to victory, with minor parties on the ideological flanks expected to hold sway over the next government's formation.\nVoting began on October 26 and ended on Saturday at 7 pm. New Zealand's Electoral Commission aimed to release a count of 5 per cent of the vote within 30 minutes of polls closing.\nResults from 95 per cent of ballots cast are expected by the end of Saturday night. A final, verified tally is due on November 27, including results from votes cast by New Zealanders living abroad.\nAnalysts said the fat",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-07T07:21:53"
    },
